/* ===== Bug bounty C: web vulnerability testing & APIs ===== */

BBT('bb-methodology', { name: 'Testing an application: the methodology', cat: 'bbweb',
  blurb: `Recon gives you hosts and endpoints; methodology turns them into bugs. The best hunters do not fire random payloads — they understand the app (its roles, objects and workflows), then test each feature against a mental checklist mapped to the OWASP Top 10. This page is the loop you run on every app, non-destructively.`,
  facts: [['Core idea', 'understand the app, then test every feature'], ['Map to', 'OWASP Top 10 / WSTG'], ['Highest ROI', 'access control & business logic'], ['Always', 'use your own test accounts']],
  cheat: [['1 · Map', 'Create accounts, click every feature, watch Burp history'], ['2 · Understand', 'Roles, objects (IDs), trust boundaries, money/data flows'], ['3 · Pick a feature', 'One workflow at a time (e.g. "share a document")'], ['4 · Ask "what if"', 'What if I change this ID? skip this step? replay this?'], ['5 · Test the class', 'Access control, injection, logic, etc. for that feature'], ['6 · Confirm & minimize', 'Reproduce, smallest PoC, no real-user data'], ['OWASP A01', 'Broken access control (IDOR/BOLA) — most common'], ['OWASP A03', 'Injection (SQLi, XSS, command)'], ['OWASP A07', 'Auth failures (session, 2FA, reset)'], ['Business logic', 'Not in scanners — think like an abuser']],
  sections: [
    ['The loop you run on every app', OL([`<b>Map it.</b> Register two accounts (A and B) and, if roles exist, one of each role. Browse every feature with Burp recording. You now have the app’s real request surface.`, `<b>Understand it.</b> Identify the <b>objects</b> (users, orders, documents — anything with an ID), the <b>roles</b> and what each should access, the <b>trust boundaries</b> (what the server must re-check), and any <b>money/data flows</b>.`, `<b>Pick one feature</b> and trace its requests. Resist jumping around; depth per feature finds more.`, `<b>Ask "what if" questions</b> and test them: change an ID, remove a parameter, skip a step, replay a request, use account A’s token on account B’s object.`, `<b>Run the relevant vuln classes</b> for that feature (the next pages: access control, auth, XSS, SSRF, injection, uploads, logic).`, `<b>Confirm, minimize, document.</b> Reproduce cleanly, build the smallest harmless PoC, and capture it for the report.`])],
    ['Map features to what usually breaks', TBL(['Feature', 'Test first for'], [['Anything with an ID in the request', 'IDOR / broken object-level access control'], ['Login, signup, password reset, 2FA', 'Auth & session flaws, reset-token issues, 2FA bypass'], ['Any field reflected back on a page', 'XSS (reflected/stored/DOM)'], ['URL/host/file-fetching features (avatars, webhooks, imports)', 'SSRF'], ['Search, filters, anything hitting a DB', 'SQL/NoSQL injection'], ['File upload / import / export', 'Upload bypass, path traversal, XXE'], ['Checkout, coupons, limits, roles', 'Business-logic & privilege flaws'], ['Templated emails/output', 'SSTI, HTML injection']])],
    ['Think like an abuser, not a scanner', PP(`Scanners test for syntactic bugs (does a quote break the query?). The money is often in <b>business logic</b>: can I apply a coupon twice, buy at a negative price, approve my own request, upgrade my role, or access a feature my plan does not include? These require understanding intent and then violating it. No tool finds them for you — your map of the app does.`)],
    ['A per-feature checklist', UL([`<b>Authorization:</b> can a lower-priv user (or no user) do this? Can I act on another user’s object by changing an ID?`, `<b>Input handling:</b> is my input reflected, stored, or used in a query, path, template, or outbound request?`, `<b>State &amp; flow:</b> can I skip, reorder, replay, or race the steps?`, `<b>Values:</b> what happens with negative, huge, zero, or someone else’s value?`, `<b>Client vs server:</b> is a check only in the UI/JS? Re-issue the raw request without it.`])]
  ],
  edge: [`Always test with accounts you create; never use another real user’s data to "prove" impact beyond the minimum.`, `Destructive tests (deleting, mass-updating, spamming) can harm the app and other users — avoid them; prove impact safely.`, `Stay in scope for every request, including the ones your tools send automatically.`, `Rate-limit your testing; a methodology loop should not look like a DoS.`, `Keep notes per feature so you do not re-test the same thing or lose a half-found bug.`],
  faq: [['Where should a beginner focus to find real bugs fastest?', PP(`Broken access control (IDOR/BOLA) and business-logic flaws. They are the most common, the least covered by scanners, and testable by hand with two accounts and Burp. Learn to change IDs and tokens systematically and to question every workflow’s assumptions.`)],
    ['How do I avoid being overwhelmed by a big app?', PP(`Go feature by feature. Pick one workflow (e.g. "invite a teammate"), trace its requests, and test it thoroughly before moving on. A complete map of one feature beats shallow pokes across fifty. Your notes track what is done.`)],
    ['Do I need to memorize payloads?', PP(`No. Understand the <i>bug classes</i> and <i>where</i> they occur; keep a payload cheat sheet (PayloadsAllTheThings) for syntax. Knowing <i>why</i> a feature is vulnerable and <i>what</i> to try matters far more than reciting payloads.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('What is the most productive first thing to do on a new app?', ['Run every scanner at once', 'Register accounts and map every feature while Burp records traffic', 'Fuzz all parameters blindly', 'Report missing security headers'], 1, 'Understanding the app’s features, roles and objects is the foundation for finding real bugs.'),
    SQ('Which class is least likely to be found by an automated scanner?', ['Reflected XSS', 'Business-logic flaws', 'Known CVEs', 'Exposed .git'], 1, 'Logic bugs require understanding intent; scanners test syntax, not business rules.')]
});
DP('bb-methodology', {
  fu: [['What is the difference between object-level and function-level access control?', 'Object-level (BOLA/IDOR): can you access another user’s specific object (order #123)? Function-level: can you call an action/endpoint your role should not (an admin API)? Test both: swap IDs for the first, call privileged endpoints as a low-priv user for the second.'],
    ['How do you keep testing non-destructive?', 'Use test accounts you own, prove impact with the minimum action (read one record, not dump the table), avoid delete/mass-update, and never touch real users’ data. If a PoC would cause harm, describe it instead of executing it.'],
    ['What is a trust boundary?', 'Any point where data crosses from less-trusted to more-trusted (client → server, one service → another). The server must re-validate authorization and input at every boundary; bugs appear where it trusts the client’s word.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Two-account diffing: perform an action as user A, capture the request, replay it as user B (Burp "Autorize" automates this). Wherever B succeeds on A’s object, you likely have broken access control.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Spraying payloads without understanding the app — high noise, mostly duplicates of scanner findings, and easy scope slips.'],
    ['In real workflows', 'Top hunters spend most time reading and understanding the app; the actual "attack" is often a single changed ID or skipped step once the mental model is right.']],
  src: ['OWASP Web Security Testing Guide (WSTG)', 'https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/'] });

BBT('bb-access', { name: 'Broken access control & IDOR', cat: 'bbweb',
  blurb: `Broken access control is the #1 web vulnerability class and the single most productive one for hunters: the server fails to check that the requester is allowed to perform an action or access an object. IDOR/BOLA — accessing another user's object by changing an identifier — is its most common form, and it is found by hand with two accounts.`,
  facts: [['OWASP rank', 'A01 — most common'], ['Core test', 'swap IDs / tokens between accounts'], ['Automate with', 'Burp Autorize / Repeater'], ['Impact', 'read/modify others’ data, privilege escalation']],
  cheat: [['IDOR / BOLA', 'Change an object ID to access another user’s object'], ['Function-level', 'Call an admin/privileged endpoint as a low-priv user'], ['Two accounts', 'A and B — act as A on B’s objects'], ['IDs to swap', 'numeric, UUID, slug, in URL/body/header/cookie'], ['Method swap', 'GET→POST/PUT/DELETE; try other verbs'], ['Missing object', 'Does it leak existence (403 vs 404)?'], ['Mass assignment', 'Add role/isAdmin fields the UI omits'], ['Autorize', 'Burp ext: replay as low-priv automatically'], ['Indirect refs', 'Even UUIDs leak via other endpoints/exports'], ['Confirm safely', 'Use your own two accounts, minimal data']],
  sections: [
    ['How to test for IDOR/BOLA', OL([`Create <b>two accounts</b>, A and B. As A, do something involving an object (view invoice, open a document, edit a profile). Capture the request in Burp.`, `Note the <b>identifier</b> (e.g. <code>/api/invoice/1001</code>, <code>?doc=uuid</code>, <code>userId</code> in the body).`, `Replay the request <b>with A’s session but B’s object ID</b> (and vice versa). If you get B’s data, that is IDOR.`, `Try <b>other identifiers</b>: numeric neighbors (1000, 1002), other users’ UUIDs you can harvest from other endpoints, exports, or shared links.`, `Try <b>method and endpoint variations</b>: change GET to POST/PUT/DELETE, add/remove an ID parameter, hit the API directly instead of via the UI.`])],
    ['Function-level access control', PP(`Beyond objects, test whether a low-privilege user can invoke <b>privileged functions</b>: admin endpoints (<code>/api/admin/...</code>), actions guarded only in the UI (a disabled button whose request you can still send), or role-restricted operations. Capture an admin action (or guess the endpoint from JS/recon) and send it as a normal user. If the server performs it, access control is broken at the function level.`)],
    ['Mass assignment (object property-level)', PP(`APIs that bind request fields straight to a model may accept fields the UI never sends. When updating your profile, try adding <code>"role":"admin"</code>, <code>"isVerified":true</code>, <code>"accountBalance":99999</code>, or <code>"userId":&lt;someone else&gt;</code> to the JSON. If the server honors them, you can escalate privileges or tamper with data it assumed you could not set.`)],
    ['Harvesting other identifiers safely', UL([`Other users’ IDs/UUIDs often leak in: team listings, shared links, exports, notification payloads, autocomplete, and API responses.`, `You only need <b>your own second account’s</b> ID to prove IDOR — do not pull real users’ data to demonstrate it.`, `For numeric IDs, testing your neighbor (your own +1 if it is your second account) proves the pattern without touching strangers.`, `Document the access-control gap, not a dump of victims’ data.`])]
  ],
  edge: [`Proving IDOR needs only your two accounts. Do not enumerate or download other real users’ records to "show impact" — that can be a crime even in scope.`, `A 403 on one path does not mean the object is protected everywhere; the same object may be reachable via another endpoint or method.`, `UUIDs are not access control — if they leak elsewhere, the object is still exposed.`, `Mass-assignment tests that set balances/roles can corrupt data; use test accounts and revert, and never touch production financial state beyond a minimal proof.`, `Automated ID enumeration is high-volume; throttle and keep it to your own objects where possible.`],
  faq: [['IDOR vs broken access control vs BOLA — same thing?', PP(`Broken access control is the umbrella. BOLA (Broken Object-Level Authorization) is the API term for accessing another user’s <i>object</i>; IDOR is the classic web name for the same idea. Function-level (BFLA) is calling privileged <i>functions</i>. All are "the server didn’t check if I’m allowed."`)],
    ['The IDs are UUIDs — is it still worth testing?', PP(`Yes. UUIDs make <i>guessing</i> hard but they are not authorization. If a UUID leaks via another endpoint, a share link, an export, or a teammate listing, you can still access the object — the server must check ownership regardless. Test with your second account’s real UUID.`)],
    ['How do I show impact without harming anyone?', PP(`Use two accounts you control. Demonstrate that account A can read or modify account B’s object. That proves the vulnerability fully. Then estimate impact in words ("any user can read any other user’s invoices") rather than by extracting real victims’ data.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('The request is GET /api/orders/5012 and you are logged in as a normal user. What is the first IDOR test?', ['Add a SQL payload to the path', 'Change 5012 to your second account’s order ID (and neighbors) and see if you receive it', 'Run nuclei', 'Report it as-is'], 1, 'Swapping the object ID while keeping your session is the core IDOR test.'),
    SQ('Updating your profile, you add "role":"admin" to the JSON and your role changes. This is…', ['Reflected XSS', 'Mass assignment (object property-level access control failure)', 'SSRF', 'Open redirect'], 1, 'The server bound a field the UI never exposes, letting you set a privileged property.')]
});
DP('bb-access', {
  fu: [['Why is access control so hard for developers to get right?', 'It must be enforced on every object and every function, server-side, for every role — a cross-cutting concern easy to miss on new endpoints. Frameworks rarely enforce ownership automatically, so each endpoint needs an explicit check, and one forgotten check is a bug.'],
    ['How does Burp Autorize speed this up?', 'You give it a low-privilege session; as you browse as a high-privilege user, it silently replays each request with the low-priv session and flags any that succeed — scaling two-account testing across the whole app.'],
    ['What makes an IDOR high vs low severity?', 'What the object is and what you can do with it: reading another user’s PII or invoices is high; modifying/deleting their data or escalating to admin is critical; accessing a non-sensitive, low-value object may be low.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Test every HTTP method and every place an ID can live (path, query, body, headers like X-User-Id, cookies). Servers often protect the GET but forget the PUT/DELETE.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Over-collecting victim data to "prove" impact. Two of your own accounts prove it; more is a legal and ethical problem.'],
    ['In real programs', 'BOLA tops the OWASP API Security Top 10 because API objects are addressed directly by ID; it is consistently among the highest-paid, most-reported classes.']],
  src: ['OWASP API Security Top 10: Broken Object Level Authorization', 'https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0xa1-broken-object-level-authorization/'] });

BBT('bb-auth', { name: 'Authentication, sessions, 2FA & password reset', cat: 'bbweb',
  blurb: `Authentication decides who you are; sessions keep you logged in; password reset and 2FA guard the edges. Each has well-known failure modes that lead straight to account takeover — the bug class programs pay well for. This page covers testing login, session handling, reset flows, 2FA, and JWTs.`,
  facts: [['Prize', 'account takeover (ATO)'], ['Weak spots', 'reset tokens, 2FA, session fixation, JWT'], ['Tools', 'Burp Repeater/Intruder, JWT Editor'], ['Test with', 'your own accounts']],
  cheat: [['Credential testing', 'Rate limiting, lockout, user enumeration on login'], ['Session', 'Rotation on login, invalidation on logout/reset, secure cookies'], ['Session fixation', 'Does the session ID change after login?'], ['Password reset', 'Token entropy, expiry, reuse, host-header poisoning'], ['2FA bypass', 'Skip step, reuse code, brute OTP, response tampering'], ['JWT', 'alg=none, weak secret, kid injection, no expiry'], ['OAuth', 'redirect_uri validation, state (CSRF), token leakage'], ['Remember-me', 'Predictable/long-lived tokens'], ['Account linking', 'Email verification bypass, pre-takeover'], ['Response tampering', 'success:false → true in client-trusted flows']],
  sections: [
    ['Login & session checks', OL([`<b>Rate limiting / lockout:</b> can you try many passwords (credential stuffing) without being throttled? Test on your own account.`, `<b>User enumeration:</b> do error messages, timing, or reset responses reveal whether an email exists?`, `<b>Session rotation:</b> capture the session cookie before and after login — a <b>fixed</b> session ID that does not change is session fixation.`, `<b>Invalidation:</b> after logout and after a password change/reset, are old sessions/tokens killed? Replay an old one to check.`, `<b>Cookie flags:</b> are session cookies <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code>, and <code>SameSite</code> set appropriately?`])],
    ['Password reset — a rich attack surface', TBL(['Test', 'What you are checking'], [['Token entropy', 'Is the reset token guessable/sequential/short?'], ['Token expiry & reuse', 'Does it expire? Can it be used twice? After login?'], ['Host header poisoning', 'Change Host/X-Forwarded-Host — does the reset link point to your domain?'], ['Password reset to any user', 'Can you set userId/email in the request to reset another account?'], ['Token leakage', 'Is the token sent to a third party (Referer, analytics)?'], ['No old-session kill', 'Do existing sessions survive a reset?']]) + PP(`Host-header poisoning in reset emails is a classic: if the app builds the reset URL from the request’s Host header, you can make the victim’s reset link point at your server and capture their token when they click.`)],
    ['2FA / OTP bypass patterns', UL([`<b>Step skipping:</b> after password, go directly to the post-2FA endpoint — is 2FA enforced server-side or just shown in the UI?`, `<b>Brute force:</b> is the OTP rate-limited? A 6-digit code with no limit is brute-forceable.`, `<b>Code reuse / no expiry:</b> does an old OTP still work? Can one code be used twice?`, `<b>Response tampering:</b> does the client trust a <code>{"verified":false}</code> it can flip to <code>true</code>?`, `<b>Backup/remember-device:</b> weak backup codes or a predictable "trust this device" token.`])],
    ['JWT testing', OL([`Decode the token (JWT Editor / jwt.io) and read the header and claims.`, `Try <b>alg confusion</b>: change <code>alg</code> to <code>none</code> or from RS256 to HS256 and sign with the public key — does the server accept it?`, `Try a <b>weak secret</b>: crack HS256 tokens offline with a wordlist (hashcat) — if the secret is weak you can forge any token.`, `Tamper with <b>claims</b> (user, role, exp) and see if they are validated server-side.`, `Check <b>expiry and revocation</b>: do expired or logged-out tokens still work?`])]
  ],
  edge: [`Test auth flaws on accounts you own; do not brute-force or reset real users’ accounts.`, `Credential-stuffing and OTP brute-force tests are high-volume and can lock out real users or look like an attack — keep them to your own accounts and mind rate rules.`, `A reset-to-any-user PoC should target your own second account, not a stranger’s.`, `Cracking a JWT secret is offline and safe; forging a token to access another user’s data crosses into their account — prove with your own identities.`, `Reporting "missing SameSite" or "no account lockout" alone is often low/informative; tie it to a concrete takeover path for impact.`],
  faq: [['What is the most impactful auth bug to look for?', PP(`Anything leading to <b>account takeover</b>: password-reset flaws (guessable/reusable tokens, host-header poisoning, reset-to-any-user), 2FA bypass, JWT forgery, and OAuth redirect_uri issues. These are consistently high-severity and well paid because they fully compromise a victim’s account.`)],
    ['How do I test 2FA without a second phone?', PP(`Most 2FA bugs are logic flaws testable with one account: enable 2FA, then try skipping the verification step (call the next endpoint directly), reusing or brute-forcing the code (your own), and tampering with the verification response. You are checking whether the server truly enforces the step, not the cryptography of the code.`)],
    ['Is "alg:none" still a real JWT bug?', PP(`Occasionally, on misconfigured or custom implementations. More common today are algorithm confusion (RS256→HS256), weak HMAC secrets you can crack offline, missing expiry/revocation, and unvalidated claims. Always decode the token, question every claim, and test whether the server validates the signature and the claims.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('The app builds the password-reset link from the request’s Host header. What attack does this enable?', ['SQL injection', 'Host-header poisoning — the victim’s reset link points to your server, leaking their token', 'Clickjacking', 'Open redirect only'], 1, 'If the reset URL trusts the Host header, you can redirect the token to a domain you control.'),
    SQ('After logging in, the session cookie value is identical to before login. This indicates…', ['Strong session security', 'Session fixation (no session rotation on authentication)', 'A JWT', 'CSRF'], 1, 'Sessions must be regenerated at login; a fixed ID lets an attacker fixate a known session.')]
});
DP('bb-auth', {
  fu: [['What is OAuth redirect_uri abuse?', 'If the authorization server accepts a redirect_uri it should not (open redirect, weak matching, extra params), the access/authorization code can be sent to an attacker-controlled URL, leading to account takeover. Also check the state parameter — missing/unused state means OAuth CSRF.'],
    ['How do you crack a JWT’s HMAC secret?', 'Offline: capture a valid HS256 token and run hashcat (mode 16500) against a wordlist. If the server used a weak/default secret, you recover it and can forge arbitrary tokens — so test only against your own token and prove forgery with your own identity.'],
    ['What is "pre-account-takeover"?', 'An attacker creates/links an account with a victim’s email before the victim signs up (e.g. via social login without email verification). When the victim later registers, the attacker may retain access. Found by testing signup, email verification and account-linking flows.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Always diff session state across the lifecycle: pre-login, post-login, post-logout, post-password-change. Replaying old tokens at each stage reveals missing invalidation.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Brute-forcing or resetting real accounts, or reporting a missing control with no takeover path (often informative). Tie findings to concrete ATO.'],
    ['In real programs', 'Password-reset and OAuth/SSO flaws are perennial high-severity payouts because they scale to any user and fully compromise accounts.']],
  src: ['OWASP WSTG: Authentication Testing', 'https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/stable/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/04-Authentication_Testing/'] });

BBT('bb-xss', { name: 'Cross-site scripting (XSS)', cat: 'bbweb',
  blurb: `XSS runs attacker-controlled JavaScript in another user's browser in the app's origin — enabling session theft, actions as the victim, and data exfiltration. It comes in reflected, stored and DOM-based forms. This page covers finding each safely, with benign proof-of-concept payloads and tools like dalfox.`,
  facts: [['Types', 'reflected, stored, DOM'], ['Impact', 'run JS as the victim in the app origin'], ['Safe PoC', 'alert(document.domain) / Collaborator ping'], ['Tools', 'Burp, dalfox, Param Miner']],
  cheat: [['Reflected', 'Input in the request is echoed into the response unsanitized'], ['Stored', 'Input saved and shown to others (worst — hits every viewer)'], ['DOM', 'Client-side JS writes your input into a sink (innerHTML, etc.)'], ['Context', 'HTML body, attribute, JS string, URL, CSS — payload differs'], ['Sinks', 'innerHTML, document.write, eval, location, dangerouslySetInnerHTML'], ['Sources', 'location.hash/search, postMessage, document.referrer'], ['Blind/stored', 'Fires elsewhere (admin panel) — use a callback'], ['Filter bypass', 'Case, encoding, broken tags, event handlers, no-script vectors'], ['PoC', 'alert(document.domain) proves origin; use OOB for blind'], ['CSP', 'May block inline JS — report but note the mitigation']],
  sections: [
    ['Find reflected XSS', OL([`Inject a <b>unique canary</b> string (e.g. <code>bbxss1337</code>) into each parameter and header, and find where it is reflected in the response.`, `Look at the <b>context</b> of the reflection: inside HTML text, an attribute value, a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block, a URL, or an event handler — each needs a different payload.`, `Try a context-appropriate <b>benign payload</b>: in HTML text, <code>&lt;svg onload=alert(document.domain)&gt;</code>; in an attribute, break out with <code>"&gt;</code> first; in JS, close the string/expression.`, `Confirm the script <b>executes</b> (an alert showing the app’s domain proves same-origin execution). Keep the PoC harmless.`])],
    ['Find stored XSS', PP(`Stored XSS is the most impactful because the payload persists and runs for everyone who views it (other users, support staff, admins). Put your canary in fields that are <b>saved and shown to others</b>: profile names, comments, support tickets, filenames, device names, anything displayed back. If it renders as HTML when another account views it, it is stored XSS. For admin-only views you cannot see, use a <b>blind XSS callback</b> (next section).`)],
    ['DOM XSS', PP(`DOM XSS happens entirely in the browser: client JS reads a <b>source</b> (<code>location.hash</code>, <code>location.search</code>, <code>postMessage</code>, <code>document.referrer</code>) and writes it to a dangerous <b>sink</b> (<code>innerHTML</code>, <code>document.write</code>, <code>eval</code>, <code>location</code>, framework HTML injectors) without sanitizing. Find it by tracing sources to sinks in the JS (Burp’s DOM Invader automates this) and crafting a URL/fragment that flows into the sink.`)],
    ['Blind & automated', UL([`<b>Blind XSS:</b> inject a payload that calls <i>your</i> collector (e.g. a Collaborator/XSS-hunter URL) so you are notified when it fires in a place you cannot see (admin dashboards, logs, ticket systems).`, `<b>dalfox:</b> automate reflected/DOM XSS discovery across many parameters/URLs, with context-aware payloads and verification.`, `<b>Param Miner:</b> find hidden parameters that may be reflected but undocumented.`, `Always verify automated hits manually and keep the final PoC benign (alert or a harmless callback).`])]
  ],
  snipTitle: 'XSS discovery (automation)', snipNote: 'Automate the search, then confirm by hand with a harmless PoC. Keep payloads non-destructive.',
  snips: [['dalfox', 'bash', `# scan a single URL's parameters for XSS
dalfox url "https://app.example.com/search?q=test"

# pipe a list of URLs with params (e.g. from gau) through dalfox
cat gau_urls.txt | grep '=' | dalfox pipe --silence

# use a custom blind-XSS callback so stored/blind hits notify you
dalfox url "https://app.example.com/profile" -b https://your-collector.example`],
    ['Benign PoC payloads', 'html', `<!-- HTML text context -->
<svg onload=alert(document.domain)>

<!-- attribute context: break out first -->
"><svg onload=alert(document.domain)>

<!-- JS string context: close the string/expr -->
';alert(document.domain)//

<!-- blind/stored: call your own collector, no harm to users -->
<script src=https://your-collector.example/x.js></script>`]],
  edge: [`Use <code>alert(document.domain)</code> or a harmless callback as proof — never real session theft against actual users, and never a self-propagating payload.`, `Self-XSS (you can only run script in your own browser, e.g. pasting into devtools) is <b>not</b> a vulnerability and most programs reject it; find a delivery path that affects another user.`, `Stored XSS PoCs run for real users who view the page — keep them benign (alert) and clean up test data afterward.`, `A strong CSP may block your payload; the bug may still be reportable but note the mitigation honestly, and don’t spend hours bypassing a solid CSP for low impact.`, `Blind-XSS callbacks must not exfiltrate real users’ data; a ping proving execution is enough.`],
  faq: [['Reflected, stored or DOM — which is most valuable?', PP(`Stored XSS is usually highest impact because it persists and runs for every viewer, including staff/admins, enabling mass account compromise. Reflected needs a victim to open a crafted link; DOM depends on client sinks. Severity ultimately depends on who can be hit and what the script can do in that origin.`)],
    ['The app reflects my input but it is HTML-encoded. Dead end?', PP(`Often, but check the exact context and encoding: encoding that is safe in HTML text may be bypassable in an attribute, a JS context, or a URL. Also try DOM sinks (client JS may use the value unsafely even if the server encodes it) and different injection points (headers, JSON, path).`)],
    ['How do I prove blind/stored XSS I cannot see fire?', PP(`Use a blind-XSS tool or a Collaborator payload that calls a server you control, optionally capturing non-sensitive context (the URL where it fired, that it is an admin page). The callback proves execution in a privileged context without you needing access to that page — and without stealing anyone’s data.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('Which XSS type typically has the highest impact and why?', ['Reflected, because it is easiest', 'Stored, because it persists and runs for every user who views the content, including admins', 'DOM, because it is client-side', 'Self-XSS, because it is reliable'], 1, 'Stored XSS reaches all viewers of the content, scaling to mass compromise.'),
    SQ('You can execute JavaScript only by pasting it into your own browser’s console. Is this a reportable XSS?', ['Yes, always critical', 'No — that is self-XSS; you need a path that affects another user', 'Yes, if you use dalfox', 'Only on weekends'], 1, 'Self-XSS has no delivery path to a victim and is generally not accepted.')]
});
DP('bb-xss', {
  fu: [['What is the difference between a source and a sink in DOM XSS?', 'A source is attacker-controllable input read by client JS (location.hash, location.search, postMessage, referrer). A sink is a dangerous API that turns a string into DOM/JS (innerHTML, document.write, eval, setAttribute on URLs). DOM XSS is a source flowing to a sink without sanitization.'],
    ['How does CSP change XSS exploitation?', 'A strict Content-Security-Policy can block inline scripts and untrusted sources, downgrading or neutralizing many payloads. Bypasses exist (unsafe-inline, JSONP endpoints, lax allowlists, dangling markup), but a solid CSP meaningfully reduces impact — report it accurately.'],
    ['Why do programs reject self-XSS?', 'Because it requires the victim to attack themselves (paste code into their own console/devtools). With no realistic delivery to another user, there is no attacker-to-victim path, so there is no real-world impact.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Match payload to context (HTML/attr/JS/URL/CSS). Burp’s DOM Invader traces sources→sinks automatically; dalfox automates reflected/DOM discovery; blind-XSS collectors catch stored payloads firing in admin tooling.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Reporting self-XSS, or chasing CSP bypasses on low-value sinks. Prove real cross-user execution and realistic impact.'],
    ['In real programs', 'Stored XSS in shared/admin-visible fields and DOM XSS via postMessage/hash are common high-value finds; impact (origin, who sees it, what it can reach) drives severity.']],
  src: ['PortSwigger Web Security Academy: Cross-site scripting', 'https://portswigger.net/web-security/cross-site-scripting'] });

BBT('bb-ssrf', { name: 'Server-side request forgery (SSRF)', cat: 'bbweb',
  blurb: `SSRF tricks the server into making HTTP (or other) requests to a destination you choose — often internal services the attacker cannot reach directly, including cloud metadata endpoints that hand out credentials. It is a high-severity favorite. This page covers finding it, confirming blind cases with out-of-band callbacks, and the cloud-metadata angle, all non-destructively.`,
  facts: [['What', 'make the server fetch a URL you control'], ['Prize', 'internal services, cloud metadata creds'], ['Blind?', 'confirm with interactsh / Collaborator'], ['Where', 'any feature that fetches a URL']],
  cheat: [['Sources', 'avatar-by-URL, webhooks, imports, PDF/HTML render, link preview, SSO metadata'], ['Full SSRF', 'Response returned to you — read internal services'], ['Blind SSRF', 'No response shown — prove via OOB callback'], ['OOB tool', 'interactsh / Burp Collaborator'], ['Cloud metadata', '169.254.169.254 (IMDS) — may leak creds'], ['Bypass filters', 'alternate IP encodings, redirects, DNS rebinding, IPv6'], ['Blocklist tricks', '[::], 0.0.0.0, decimal/octal IPs, @ tricks'], ['Protocols', 'sometimes file://, gopher:// expand impact'], ['Impact', 'internal recon, metadata creds, pivot'], ['Confirm safely', 'hit your own OOB host or a benign internal check']],
  sections: [
    ['Where SSRF lives', PP(`Look for any feature where the <b>server fetches a URL or resource on your behalf</b>: "import from URL", avatar/image by URL, webhooks and callback URLs, link/preview unfurling, PDF or screenshot generators, document/HTML converters, RSS/feed importers, and SSO/SAML metadata URLs. Anywhere you supply something the server will request is an SSRF candidate.`)],
    ['Test it step by step', OL([`Point the fetch at a <b>URL you control</b> (an interactsh/Collaborator host). If the server contacts it, the feature makes server-side requests — SSRF is in play.`, `Try an <b>internal target</b> that is safe to probe: <code>http://127.0.0.1/</code>, <code>http://localhost/</code>, common internal ports. Full SSRF returns the internal response to you; blind SSRF shows nothing in the response (rely on the OOB callback).`, `If cloud-hosted, test the <b>metadata endpoint</b> carefully (see below) — but do not harvest or use any credentials.`, `If blocked, try <b>filter bypasses</b>: alternate IP encodings, a redirect from your server to the internal target, IPv6 (<code>[::1]</code>), or DNS rebinding.`, `Document the reachable internal surface as proof; do not pivot or exfiltrate.`])],
    ['Cloud metadata (high impact) — handle with care', PP(`On cloud VMs, a link-local metadata service at <code>169.254.169.254</code> serves instance info and, if misconfigured (e.g. IMDSv1), temporary <b>credentials</b>. Reaching it via SSRF can mean reading those credentials — a critical finding. Confirm you can reach the metadata path and capture the <b>minimum</b> proof (that the endpoint is reachable / a non-secret field). <b>Do not</b> retrieve, display, or use actual credentials or customer data; describe the impact and let the program verify. Using recovered cloud creds is unauthorized access.`)],
    ['Confirming blind SSRF', UL([`Inject a unique <b>interactsh/Collaborator</b> URL; a DNS or HTTP hit proves the server made the request even when the response shows nothing.`, `Distinguish <b>DNS-only</b> (resolver looked it up) from <b>full HTTP</b> callbacks — HTTP interaction is stronger proof and usually higher impact.`, `Use the callback to show the server can reach <b>external and internal</b> destinations; combine with internal port probing (carefully) to demonstrate reach.`, `Keep all callbacks to infrastructure you own.`])]
  ],
  snipTitle: 'SSRF confirmation (out-of-band)', snipNote: 'Prove the server makes requests using a host you control. Never fetch or use real credentials/data.',
  snips: [['interactsh (blind SSRF)', 'bash', `# start a listener that records DNS/HTTP callbacks to a unique domain
interactsh-client -v
# it prints a unique URL like: abc123.oast.fun

# then set the app's "fetch URL" / webhook / avatar-url field to:
#   http://abc123.oast.fun/ssrf-test
# a callback in interactsh = the server made the request = SSRF`],
    ['Common test targets (benign)', 'text', `# prove the server fetches YOUR url (OOB):
http://<your-interactsh>.oast.fun/

# safe internal reachability checks:
http://127.0.0.1/
http://localhost:80/
http://[::1]/

# cloud metadata (confirm reachability ONLY; do not read/use creds):
http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/      # AWS IMDS
# filter-bypass encodings to try if blocked:
http://127.1/   http://0x7f000001/   http://2130706433/`]],
  edge: [`Cloud metadata can expose live credentials — confirm reachability with the minimum proof and never retrieve, display, or use the credentials; doing so is unauthorized access.`, `Keep OOB callbacks to hosts you own; do not use third-party webhook catchers that leak data.`, `Do not use SSRF to pivot, scan broadly, or read internal data beyond a minimal proof — that can cause harm and exceed authorization.`, `Some "SSRF" is intended (the app is supposed to fetch arbitrary URLs); impact comes from reaching internal/metadata targets, so show that, not just an external callback.`, `Heavy internal port scanning via SSRF is noisy and risky; prove reach, don’t map the whole network.`],
  faq: [['Full SSRF vs blind SSRF — what is the difference?', PP(`Full (or "basic") SSRF returns the fetched response to you, so you can read internal services directly. Blind SSRF gives no response in the app, so you confirm it via an out-of-band callback (interactsh/Collaborator) and infer impact. Full SSRF is easier to exploit and demonstrate; blind SSRF can still be high impact if it reaches internal/metadata targets.`)],
    ['How do I show SSRF impact without crossing the line?', PP(`Prove the server makes requests you control (OOB callback) and that it can reach an internal/metadata target (reachability, minimal proof). Describe what that enables ("can reach the cloud metadata service / internal admin on :8080"). Do <b>not</b> extract credentials, read internal data, or pivot — that exceeds authorization and can cause harm. Reachability plus a clear impact narrative is a strong report.`)],
    ['Why is 169.254.169.254 special?', PP(`It is the link-local cloud metadata endpoint. Depending on configuration, it exposes instance metadata and sometimes temporary IAM/role credentials. SSRF that reaches it can escalate to cloud account compromise — which is why it is a critical target. Confirm reachability only; never use any credentials you could retrieve.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('A "fetch avatar from URL" feature contacts your interactsh host when you supply its URL. What have you found?', ['Reflected XSS', 'SSRF — the server makes requests to URLs you control', 'IDOR', 'Open redirect'], 1, 'The server making an out-of-band request to your URL confirms SSRF.'),
    SQ('Your SSRF can reach 169.254.169.254. What is the correct, in-scope way to handle it?', ['Download the IAM credentials and use them to prove account takeover', 'Confirm reachability with minimal proof and report; do not retrieve or use credentials', 'Ignore it; metadata is not sensitive', 'Scan the whole internal network to map impact'], 1, 'Reaching metadata is critical to report, but retrieving/using creds or pivoting exceeds authorization.')]
});
DP('bb-ssrf', {
  fu: [['What is DNS rebinding and how does it bypass SSRF filters?', 'You control a domain that first resolves to an allowed IP (passing the app’s check) then re-resolves to an internal IP (169.254.169.254 or 127.0.0.1) for the actual request. The time gap between validation and use lets the destination change — defeating naive allowlist checks.'],
    ['IMDSv1 vs IMDSv2 — why does it matter?', 'IMDSv1 answers simple GETs, so SSRF can read credentials directly. IMDSv2 requires a session token obtained via a PUT with a header, which most SSRF cannot send — mitigating credential theft. Finding IMDSv1 reachable is more severe.'],
    ['Can SSRF use non-HTTP protocols?', 'Sometimes. If the fetcher accepts file://, gopher://, dict:// etc., impact grows (read local files, talk to internal TCP services). Test which schemes the feature accepts — but keep proofs minimal and non-destructive.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Chain OOB confirmation (interactsh) with careful internal reachability proof. For filtered targets, try alternate IP encodings, redirect-based bypass, and IPv6 before concluding it is not exploitable.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Retrieving/using cloud credentials or deep-scanning internal networks — crossing from "proven SSRF" into unauthorized access and potential harm.'],
    ['In real programs', 'SSRF-to-cloud-metadata has caused major breaches; programs pay highly for it. Blind SSRF confirmed via OOB with a clear internal-reach narrative is a strong, common report.']],
  src: ['PortSwigger Web Security Academy: SSRF', 'https://portswigger.net/web-security/ssrf'] });

BBT('bb-injection', { name: 'Injection: SQL, NoSQL, command & template', cat: 'bbweb',
  blurb: `Injection happens when your input is placed into a query, command, or template without separation, so it changes the code the server runs. SQL injection is the classic; NoSQL, OS-command and server-side template injection follow the same idea. This page covers detecting each safely and using sqlmap responsibly.`,
  facts: [['OWASP', 'A03 — injection'], ['Classic', 'SQL injection (SQLi)'], ['Others', 'NoSQL, OS command, SSTI, LDAP'], ['Tool', 'sqlmap (carefully)']],
  cheat: [['SQLi detect', "Break the query: ' , '' , ) , --  then observe errors/diffs"], ['SQLi types', 'Error-based, UNION, boolean-blind, time-blind'], ['NoSQL', 'Operator injection: {"$ne":null}, {"$gt":""}'], ['Command injection', '; | && $(...) `...` — OOB-confirm blind cases'], ['SSTI', '{{7*7}} / ${7*7} → 49 means template evaluates input'], ['sqlmap', 'Automated SQLi detection/exploitation'], ['Confirm', 'Reproduce minimally; do not dump real data'], ['Blind', 'Time delays or OOB callbacks prove it'], ['WAF', 'May block payloads; tamper/encoding sometimes bypass'], ['Impact', 'data read/modify, RCE (command/SSTI)']],
  sections: [
    ['SQL injection — detect then confirm', OL([`<b>Probe</b> each input that likely hits the database (search, filters, id, sort, login) with query-breaking characters: a single quote <code>'</code>, then <code>''</code>, then <code>)</code> and comment sequences. A database error or a changed response is a signal.`, `<b>Confirm logically</b> with boolean pairs: a condition that is true (<code>' OR '1'='1</code>) vs false (<code>' OR '1'='2</code>) producing different responses indicates injection — without extracting data.`, `For <b>blind</b> cases, use a <b>time delay</b> (a sleep function) or an <b>OOB</b> callback to confirm execution when the response does not change.`, `<b>Prove minimally:</b> demonstrate the injection (e.g. the DB version or that a boolean condition is honored). Do <b>not</b> dump tables of real user data to show impact.`])],
    ['Using sqlmap responsibly', PP(`sqlmap automates detecting and exploiting SQLi. Point it at a request you captured (save it from Burp), let it <b>confirm</b> the injection and identify the DBMS, and stop at proof. Use low risk/level settings first, respect rate limits, and <b>never</b> use it to exfiltrate real customer data — enumerating the current database name or version is enough to prove the bug. Dumping real PII to "show impact" is harmful and can exceed authorization.`)],
    ['NoSQL, command & template injection', TBL(['Type', 'How to detect', 'Benign proof'], [['NoSQL (e.g. Mongo)', 'Inject operators in JSON/params', '{"$ne":null} bypasses a filter/login logic'], ['OS command', 'Append ;, |, &&, $(), backticks', 'A sleep/ping to your OOB host (no destructive commands)'], ['SSTI', 'Inject template syntax into fields reflected server-side', '{{7*7}} or ${7*7} returns 49'], ['LDAP', 'Inject *, ), | into LDAP queries', 'A filter that returns more/all entries']]) + PP(`Command injection and SSTI can lead to <b>remote code execution</b> — prove them with the lightest possible evidence (a math result for SSTI, an OOB ping for blind command injection), never a reverse shell or destructive command on a live target.`)],
    ['Blind confirmation without extraction', UL([`<b>Time-based:</b> a payload that makes the server pause (e.g. a DB sleep) proves execution by the delay — no data read.`, `<b>Out-of-band:</b> make the server resolve/request your interactsh host from within the query/command.`, `<b>Boolean:</b> true vs false conditions yielding different responses prove control of the query logic.`, `All three demonstrate the vulnerability without dumping sensitive data.`])]
  ],
  snipTitle: 'Injection detection', snipNote: 'Detect and confirm minimally. Do not exfiltrate real data or run destructive commands.',
  snips: [['sqlmap (confirm only)', 'bash', `# save the request from Burp (right-click → Copy to file) as req.txt, then:
sqlmap -r req.txt --batch --level 2 --risk 1 --banner
# prove the bug minimally: current DB name / user — NOT a full dump
sqlmap -r req.txt --batch --current-db --current-user
# stop here for a PoC; do not --dump real user tables`],
    ['Benign detection payloads', 'text', `# SQLi signals (observe errors / response differences):
'          ''          ')          --  -
' OR '1'='1       vs      ' OR '1'='2       (boolean pair)
# blind proof (time) — use the DBMS's sleep; delay confirms execution

# NoSQL operator injection (JSON body):
{"username":{"$ne":null},"password":{"$ne":null}}

# SSTI probes (if it returns 49, the template evaluates input):
{{7*7}}        ${7*7}        #{7*7}        <%= 7*7 %>

# blind OS command injection — OOB ping to a host YOU own (no destructive cmd):
; nslookup <your-interactsh>.oast.fun`]],
  edge: [`Confirm injection with the lightest proof (version, boolean, delay, OOB). Dumping real customer data is harmful and can exceed authorization — do not do it.`, `sqlmap at high level/risk or with --dump/--os-shell can be destructive and heavy; keep settings low and stop at confirmation.`, `Command injection and SSTI can give RCE — never run reverse shells, read system files beyond a trivial proof, or modify the server.`, `Time-based payloads add load; keep delays short and requests few.`, `A WAF block is not proof of safety; note it, but do not hammer it with thousands of bypass attempts (noisy, possible DoS).`],
  faq: [['How do I prove SQLi without dumping the database?', PP(`Use a boolean pair (true vs false conditions giving different responses), a short time delay, or an OOB callback, and optionally retrieve a non-sensitive fact like the DB version or current database name. That fully demonstrates the injection. Extracting real user tables adds no validity and causes harm — describe the potential impact instead.`)],
    ['Is it safe to run sqlmap on a live program target?', PP(`Within scope and at low level/risk, using it to <i>confirm</i> and identify the DBMS, yes — it is a standard tool. Keep rates modest, avoid <code>--dump</code> of real data, <code>--os-shell</code>, and other aggressive options, and respect the program’s automation rules. The goal is proof, not exploitation.`)],
    ['How does SSTI differ from XSS?', PP(`XSS runs in the <i>victim’s browser</i>; SSTI executes in the <i>server’s template engine</i> and can lead to server-side code execution. The giveaway is a math expression in template syntax (<code>{{7*7}}</code>) being evaluated to <code>49</code> in the server’s response. Because it can reach RCE, prove it with the lightest evidence only.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('Which is a correct, non-destructive way to confirm SQL injection?', ['Dump all user tables to prove impact', 'Use a boolean true/false pair or a short time delay and read the DB version', 'Delete a row and see if it is gone', 'Run --os-shell immediately'], 1, 'Minimal proofs confirm the bug without harming data.'),
    SQ('A field renders {{7*7}} as 49 in the server response. This indicates…', ['Reflected XSS', 'Server-side template injection (SSTI)', 'Open redirect', 'IDOR'], 1, 'The server-side template engine evaluated your input — SSTI, which can lead to RCE.')]
});
DP('bb-injection', {
  fu: [['What are the four main SQLi techniques?', 'Error-based (the DB error leaks data), UNION-based (append a UNION SELECT to read other data), boolean-blind (infer bits from true/false response differences), and time-blind (infer bits from induced delays). Blind techniques prove the bug without needing visible output.'],
    ['Why is command injection often tested out-of-band?', 'Many command-injection points show no output ("blind"). Making the server perform a DNS/HTTP request to your interactsh host (e.g. via nslookup/curl in the payload) proves code execution without needing a visible result or a destructive action.'],
    ['How do WAFs affect injection testing?', 'WAFs block known payload patterns. Encoding, comments, case changes and sqlmap tamper scripts sometimes bypass them, but a WAF reducing exploitability should be noted honestly; don’t flood it with thousands of attempts (noisy, possible DoS).']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Prefer the least-invasive confirmation: boolean/time/OOB over data extraction. For SSTI/command injection, a math result or a single OOB callback proves RCE potential without touching the system.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Dumping real data or spawning shells to "prove" severity — unnecessary for validity and a serious ethical/legal problem.'],
    ['In real programs', 'SQLi is rarer than it used to be (ORMs/parameterized queries) but still high-paying when found; SSTI and command injection are prized for their RCE potential.']],
  src: ['PortSwigger Web Security Academy: SQL injection', 'https://portswigger.net/web-security/sql-injection'] });

BBT('bb-files', { name: 'File upload & path traversal / LFI', cat: 'bbweb',
  blurb: `Features that accept files or build file paths from input are a rich source of high-severity bugs: upload bypasses can lead to stored XSS or code execution, and path traversal / local file inclusion can read sensitive files off the server. This page covers testing both safely.`,
  facts: [['Upload risks', 'XSS, SSRF, overwrite, code execution'], ['Path risks', 'read arbitrary files (../../etc/passwd)'], ['Key checks', 'extension, content-type, path, storage location'], ['Prove', 'minimal, non-destructive']],
  cheat: [['Upload — extension', 'Bypass allowlist: double ext, case, null byte, alt extensions'], ['Upload — content-type', 'Spoof MIME; rely on magic bytes vs declared type'], ['Upload — content', 'SVG/HTML → stored XSS; polyglots'], ['Upload — path', 'Control filename/path → overwrite or traversal'], ['Upload — server exec', 'If stored in a web-exec dir, a script may run (high)'], ['Path traversal', '../ sequences reach files outside the intended dir'], ['LFI', 'App includes a file chosen by input → read/execute'], ['Encodings', '..%2f, ..%252f, ....//, absolute paths'], ['Benign proof', 'read a known-safe file; show one line'], ['Defenses', 'allowlist ext, random names, non-exec storage, canonicalize paths']],
  sections: [
    ['Testing file upload', OL([`Upload a <b>normal file</b> first and watch the request/response: note how the <b>filename</b>, <b>content-type</b> and resulting <b>URL/path</b> are handled.`, `Test the <b>extension control</b>: try alternate/representative extensions, double extensions (<code>file.png.html</code>), case variations, and trailing tricks to slip past an allowlist.`, `Test <b>content vs type</b>: upload an <b>SVG or HTML</b> file (which can carry script) with an image content-type — if it is served inline from the app origin, that is stored XSS.`, `Test the <b>stored path</b>: can you influence where it lands (traversal in the filename) or <b>overwrite</b> an existing file?`, `If files land in a <b>script-executable</b> directory and the server runs them, that can be code execution — prove it with the lightest benign evidence and report immediately; do not plant a real webshell on production.`])],
    ['What makes an upload bug high severity', TBL(['Outcome', 'Severity driver'], [['Stored XSS via SVG/HTML', 'Runs script for viewers in the app origin'], ['Server-side execution', 'Uploaded file is executed → RCE (critical)'], ['Path traversal on store', 'Overwrite config/other users’ files'], ['SSRF via file fetch', 'Server fetches a remote "file" URL'], ['DoS / resource', 'Huge files, zip bombs (don’t actually DoS — describe)']])],
    ['Path traversal & local file inclusion', OL([`Find inputs that look like <b>file names or paths</b>: <code>?file=report.pdf</code>, <code>?template=home</code>, download/export endpoints, image/doc loaders.`, `Inject <b>traversal sequences</b> toward a known-safe file to prove you escape the intended directory (and try encodings if filtered: <code>..%2f</code>, <code>..%252f</code>, <code>....//</code>).`, `A benign proof is reading a <b>non-sensitive, well-known</b> file and showing a single identifying line — enough to prove traversal without harvesting secrets.`, `If the app <b>includes/executes</b> the referenced file (LFI), impact rises (possible code execution via log poisoning, wrappers) — prove minimally and report; do not weaponize.`])],
    ['Proving impact without harm', UL([`For upload-to-XSS, a benign <code>alert(document.domain)</code> in the SVG/HTML, viewed from the app origin, is proof.`, `For traversal, show you can reach one known file (a single line), not a dump of <code>/etc/shadow</code> or app secrets.`, `For execution, a file that returns a harmless marker proves it — never a functional webshell or anything that lets others in.`, `Clean up uploaded test files where you can, and report promptly.`])]
  ],
  edge: [`Never plant a working webshell or persistent backdoor on a live target — prove execution with a harmless marker and report immediately; a real shell is both harmful and likely a crime.`, `Traversal proofs should read a known, non-sensitive file (one line) — do not harvest server secrets, keys, or other users’ files.`, `Uploaded test files can be visible to real users (stored XSS) — keep them benign and remove them afterward.`, `Zip bombs / huge uploads can DoS the service — describe the risk, don’t actually trigger it.`, `Overwrite tests can corrupt real files; target your own test artifacts, not shared/production files.`],
  faq: [['Why is an SVG upload dangerous if "it is just an image"?', PP(`SVG is XML that can contain <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>. If the app stores it and serves it <b>inline from its own origin</b> (not as an attachment or from a sandboxed domain), viewing it executes the script in the app’s origin — stored XSS. HTML uploads served inline behave the same way. The fix is to serve such files as downloads or from an isolated origin.`)],
    ['How do I prove path traversal responsibly?', PP(`Demonstrate that you can escape the intended directory by reading a single, well-known, non-sensitive file and showing one identifying line. That proves the vulnerability. Do not enumerate or exfiltrate secrets, credentials, or other users’ data to "increase impact" — the traversal itself is the finding.`)],
    ['Upload leads to code execution — should I drop a webshell?', PP(`No. Prove it with the lightest possible evidence — a file that returns a harmless, unique marker when executed — then report immediately and remove it if you can. A functional webshell endangers the target and its users and can be a criminal act even in scope. Minimal proof is both safer and sufficient.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('You can upload an SVG that the app serves inline from its own domain, and it runs script. This is…', ['SSRF', 'Stored XSS via file upload', 'Path traversal', 'A non-issue — SVGs are images'], 1, 'Inline-served SVG/HTML can execute script in the app origin, i.e. stored XSS.'),
    SQ('What is a responsible proof of path traversal?', ['Dump /etc/shadow and all app secrets', 'Read one line of a known non-sensitive file to show you escaped the directory', 'Delete the target file', 'Upload a webshell'], 1, 'Minimal proof demonstrates the bug without harvesting sensitive data.')]
});
DP('bb-files', {
  fu: [['Why does the storage location matter so much for uploads?', 'If files are stored in a directory the web server will execute (e.g. a scripts path) and keep their extension, an uploaded script can run — RCE. If stored in a non-executable path or an isolated origin with random names, the same upload is far less dangerous. Always determine where and how the file is served.'],
    ['What encodings bypass naive traversal filters?', 'URL-encoding (..%2f), double-encoding (..%252f), mixed/variant sequences (....//), absolute paths, and sometimes Unicode variants. A filter that strips one literal "../" often misses these.'],
    ['What is log poisoning in LFI?', 'If an app can include a file whose contents you can influence (like a log the server writes your input to), you may get code execution by planting code in the log then including it. It is advanced; prove the inclusion primitive minimally and report rather than fully weaponizing.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'For uploads, test the three controls independently (extension, declared type, actual content/path) — bypassing any one can be enough. For traversal, escalate encodings before concluding it is filtered.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Planting real webshells, dumping sensitive files, or overwriting production data to "prove" severity — harmful and often criminal. Minimal benign proof suffices.'],
    ['In real programs', 'Upload-to-stored-XSS and upload-to-RCE are common high/critical findings; path traversal that reaches config/secret files is high severity — proven with one line, not a dump.']],
  src: ['OWASP: File Upload & Path Traversal (WSTG)', 'https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/stable/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/'] });

BBT('bb-logic', { name: 'Business logic, CSRF, CORS & open redirect', cat: 'bbweb',
  blurb: `Not every bug is an injection. Business-logic flaws abuse the app's intended workflow (coupons, limits, prices, roles); CSRF makes a victim's browser perform actions unknowingly; misconfigured CORS leaks data cross-origin; open redirects enable phishing and chain into bigger bugs. These are high-value precisely because scanners miss them.`,
  facts: [['Logic bugs', 'abuse intended workflow — no scanner finds them'], ['CSRF', 'victim’s browser performs an action unknowingly'], ['CORS', 'misconfig leaks responses cross-origin'], ['Open redirect', 'phishing + chains into OAuth/SSRF']],
  cheat: [['Business logic', 'Coupon reuse, negative qty/price, limit bypass, role/plan bypass, step skipping'], ['Race conditions', 'Fire parallel requests (double-spend, reuse)'], ['CSRF', 'State-changing request with no anti-CSRF token / only cookie auth'], ['CSRF test', 'Remove/alter token; check SameSite; craft a cross-site request'], ['CORS', 'ACAO reflects origin + ACAC true → credentialed cross-origin read'], ['Open redirect', '?next=, ?url=, ?redirect= → external site'], ['Chain', 'Open redirect → OAuth token theft / SSRF filter bypass'], ['Clickjacking', 'Sensitive action framable (no frame-ancestors)'], ['Prove', 'minimal PoC, your own accounts'], ['Impact', 'money, privilege, data — argue it clearly']],
  sections: [
    ['Business-logic testing: break the intended rules', OL([`List the app’s <b>rules and assumptions</b> for a workflow: "a coupon is used once", "price is positive", "only paid plans access X", "you approve others’ requests, not your own", "quantity ≤ stock".`, `For each rule, craft a request that <b>violates it</b>: apply the coupon twice (or in parallel), set a <b>negative</b> or <b>zero</b> price/quantity, call a paid-feature endpoint on a free plan, approve your own request, exceed a limit.`, `Test <b>step skipping and reordering</b>: jump to the final step (payment confirmation, order completion) without the prerequisites.`, `Test <b>race conditions</b>: fire the same request many times in parallel (Turbo Intruder) to use a one-time action twice — redeem a balance/coupon/invite more than once.`, `Prove with your own accounts and <b>quantify impact</b> ("buy any item for $0", "unlimited coupon stacking", "free access to premium").`])],
    ['CSRF', PP(`CSRF abuses the fact that browsers attach cookies automatically: a malicious page makes the victim’s browser send a <b>state-changing</b> request (change email, transfer funds) to the app, authenticated by the victim’s cookies. Test by taking a sensitive request and checking whether it is protected: is there an <b>anti-CSRF token</b> that is validated? Is <code>SameSite</code> set on the session cookie? Does it rely on a custom header? Remove/alter the token and replay; if it still works and can be triggered cross-site, build a minimal HTML PoC that performs the action. Note that <code>SameSite=Lax</code> defaults mitigate many classic CSRFs — argue the real exploitability.`)],
    ['CORS misconfiguration', PP(`CORS controls which origins can <i>read</i> cross-origin responses. The dangerous misconfig: the server <b>reflects the request’s Origin</b> into <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> <i>and</i> sets <code>Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true</code>. Then any site can make the victim’s browser fetch their authenticated data and read it. Test by sending an <code>Origin: https://evil.example</code> header and checking whether it is reflected with credentials allowed. Prove it by reading your own account’s data from a different origin; do not harvest other users’.`)],
    ['Open redirect & chaining', UL([`Find redirect parameters (<code>?next=</code>, <code>?url=</code>, <code>?redirect=</code>, <code>returnTo</code>) and test whether they accept an <b>external</b> URL.`, `On its own, an open redirect is usually low severity (phishing) — but it <b>chains</b>: it can steal <b>OAuth tokens</b> (if used as a redirect_uri), bypass <b>SSRF</b> allowlists, or make a trusted link lead to an attacker page.`, `Prove by redirecting to a harmless external page you control; then describe the chain for real impact.`, `Also check <b>clickjacking</b>: can a sensitive action be framed (no <code>frame-ancestors</code>/X-Frame-Options)? Only report where framing enables a real action.`])]
  ],
  snipTitle: 'CSRF / CORS proof snippets', snipNote: 'Minimal PoCs. Trigger actions only on your own test accounts.',
  snips: [['CSRF PoC (HTML)', 'html', `<!-- hosted on attacker page; if it changes the victim's email, CSRF is present -->
<form action="https://app.example.com/account/email" method="POST">
  <input type="hidden" name="email" value="attacker@evil.example">
</form>
<script>document.forms[0].submit()</script>
<!-- test first by removing/altering any csrf token and checking SameSite on the session cookie -->`],
    ['CORS test', 'bash', `# does the server reflect an arbitrary Origin AND allow credentials?
curl -s -I https://app.example.com/api/me \\
  -H "Origin: https://evil.example" \\
  -H "Cookie: session=<your-own-session>" | \\
  grep -i 'access-control-allow-'
# vulnerable if: Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.example
#            AND Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`]],
  edge: [`Logic and race tests can move real money or state — use your own accounts, small amounts, and revert; never exploit real transactions at scale.`, `CSRF with modern SameSite=Lax defaults is often not exploitable for top-level POSTs; verify a real cross-site path before claiming it, or it will be closed as informative.`, `CORS and CSRF PoCs must act on your own account’s data — do not read or change other users’.`, `Open redirect alone is frequently low/informative; land the impact by demonstrating a chain (OAuth/SSRF/phishing of a sensitive flow).`, `Clickjacking reports need a sensitive, framable action to be accepted — framing a static page is not impactful.`],
  faq: [['Why do business-logic bugs pay well?', PP(`Because they are unique to the app, invisible to scanners, and often have direct financial impact (free purchases, coupon stacking, limit bypass, privilege escalation). They require understanding the app’s intent and then violating it — exactly the skill that distinguishes good hunters. There is no signature to scan for.`)],
    ['Is CSRF still relevant with SameSite cookies?', PP(`Less than before, but yes in specific cases: when cookies are <code>SameSite=None</code>, when the action uses a method/flow SameSite does not cover, when the app relies on a token it does not actually validate, or for login/logout CSRF and GET-based state changes. Always verify a realistic cross-site exploitation path rather than assuming — and argue it in the report.`)],
    ['What turns a low open redirect into a real bug?', PP(`The chain. Used as an OAuth <code>redirect_uri</code>, an open redirect can leak the authorization code/token (account takeover). It can bypass SSRF allowlists (your allowed URL redirects to an internal target). It makes phishing links originate from the trusted domain. Report the open redirect <i>with</i> the concrete chain for severity.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('You can apply a single-use coupon many times by sending the redeem request in parallel. This is a…', ['Reflected XSS', 'Race condition / business-logic flaw', 'CORS misconfiguration', 'Open redirect'], 1, 'Parallel requests defeating a one-time check is a classic race-condition logic bug.'),
    SQ('A server responds with Access-Control-Allow-Origin reflecting your Origin AND Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true. Impact?', ['None, this is normal', 'Any origin can read the victim’s authenticated responses cross-origin', 'It prevents CSRF', 'It only affects images'], 1, 'Reflecting arbitrary origins with credentials allowed lets malicious sites read authenticated data.')]
});
DP('bb-logic', {
  fu: [['How do you test a race condition in Burp?', 'Use Turbo Intruder or Repeater’s parallel send (the single-packet attack) to fire many copies of a request near-simultaneously, so they hit the server before a one-time check updates state — proving double-redeem, double-spend or limit bypass.'],
    ['When is open redirect actually medium/high?', 'When chained: as an OAuth redirect_uri to steal tokens (account takeover), to bypass an SSRF allowlist, or to make a security-sensitive flow (password reset, SSO) land on an attacker page. Standalone, it is usually low/phishing.'],
    ['What is the difference between CSRF and CORS bugs?', 'CSRF makes the victim’s browser perform a state-changing action (write) using their cookies. CORS misconfig lets a malicious origin read cross-origin responses (read). One abuses implicit authentication to act; the other to read data.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'For logic bugs, write down the invariant the developer assumed, then break it (negative, zero, duplicate, parallel, skipped step). The bug is the gap between intended and enforced rules.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'Reporting SameSite-mitigated CSRF, standalone open redirects, or framing of static pages as high severity — common informative closes. Always show a real exploit path.'],
    ['In real programs', 'Race conditions (double-spend, coupon/invite reuse) and plan/role bypasses are increasingly reported and well paid as tooling (Turbo Intruder, single-packet attack) made them easy to demonstrate.']],
  src: ['PortSwigger Web Security Academy: Business logic vulnerabilities', 'https://portswigger.net/web-security/logic-flaws'] });

BBT('bb-api', { name: 'Testing APIs: REST & GraphQL', cat: 'bbapi',
  blurb: `Most modern apps are API-first, and the API often has more surface and weaker authorization than the web UI. REST APIs expose objects directly by ID (BOLA heaven); GraphQL exposes a flexible query language with its own pitfalls (introspection, nested abuse, missing field-level auth). This page covers testing both.`,
  facts: [['API Top 10', 'OWASP API Security Top 10'], ['#1 API bug', 'BOLA (object-level auth)'], ['REST surface', 'versions, methods, hidden endpoints'], ['GraphQL', 'introspection, nested queries, field auth']],
  cheat: [['Find the API', 'JS, mobile app traffic, /api docs, swagger/openapi.json'], ['BOLA/BFLA', 'Swap object IDs; call privileged endpoints as low-priv'], ['Methods', 'Try GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE/OPTIONS per endpoint'], ['Versions', 'Old /v1 may lack fixes present in /v2'], ['Mass assignment', 'Send extra fields (role, isAdmin)'], ['Rate/limits', 'Missing limits → enumeration, brute'], ['GraphQL introspection', 'Dump the schema to learn every query/mutation'], ['GraphQL tools', 'graphw00f (detect), clairvoyance (schema), InQL (Burp)'], ['GraphQL abuse', 'Batching, deep nesting (DoS), field-level auth gaps'], ['Docs', 'Swagger/OpenAPI reveals params & endpoints']],
  sections: [
    ['Find and map the API', OL([`Discover endpoints from <b>JS</b> (jsluice/katana), the <b>mobile app</b> (proxy its traffic through Burp), and <b>docs</b> (<code>/swagger</code>, <code>/openapi.json</code>, <code>/graphql</code>, <code>/api/docs</code>).`, `Import an <b>OpenAPI/Swagger</b> spec into Burp/Postman to get every endpoint, method and parameter at once.`, `Enumerate <b>versions</b> (<code>/v1</code>, <code>/v2</code>, <code>/internal</code>) — older versions may lack authorization fixes.`, `For each endpoint, note the <b>objects</b> (IDs) and <b>required role</b> — your authorization test matrix.`])],
    ['REST API: the high-value tests', TBL(['Test', 'What to do'], [['BOLA (object-level)', 'Swap object IDs across your two accounts (the #1 API bug)'], ['BFLA (function-level)', 'Call admin/privileged endpoints as a normal/no-auth user'], ['Method tampering', 'Try other verbs; a GET may be protected but PUT/DELETE not'], ['Mass assignment', 'Add fields like role, isAdmin, ownerId to the JSON'], ['Excessive data exposure', 'Does the response include fields the UI hides (other users’ PII)?'], ['Missing rate limits', 'Enables enumeration, brute force, resource abuse'], ['Old versions', 'Re-test known-fixed bugs against /v1']]) + PP(`APIs frequently return <b>more data than the UI shows</b> — check raw responses for fields (emails, tokens, internal flags) the front-end just hides.`)],
    ['GraphQL specifics', OL([`<b>Detect & fingerprint:</b> find the <code>/graphql</code> endpoint; graphw00f identifies the engine.`, `<b>Introspection:</b> if enabled, query the schema to list <b>every</b> query, mutation and type — a full map of the API. If disabled, clairvoyance can often reconstruct it.`, `<b>Authorization per field/mutation:</b> GraphQL auth is often inconsistent — a field or mutation may lack the check its REST equivalent has. Test sensitive queries/mutations as a low-priv user.`, `<b>Abuse the query language:</b> deeply <b>nested</b> queries and <b>batching</b> (many operations in one request) can cause DoS or bypass rate limits — demonstrate carefully without actually downing the service.`, `<b>Use InQL</b> (Burp) to parse the schema and generate queries to test quickly.`])],
    ['Why APIs are so productive', UL([`Objects are addressed <b>directly by ID</b>, so BOLA is everywhere if ownership checks are missing.`, `APIs evolve fast; <b>new endpoints</b> and versions ship without complete authorization.`, `Mobile and SPA backends expose endpoints the web UI never surfaces.`, `Responses often <b>over-return</b> data, leaking fields the UI hides.`])]
  ],
  snipTitle: 'API & GraphQL testing', snipNote: 'Map and test with your own accounts. Avoid heavy nested/batched queries that could DoS.',
  snips: [['Find & map', 'bash', `# common API/doc locations to check
#   /swagger.json  /openapi.json  /api/docs  /v1  /v2  /graphql
curl -s https://app.example.com/openapi.json | jq '.paths | keys'

# GraphQL: detect the engine, then (if allowed) introspect the schema
graphw00f -t https://app.example.com/graphql
# introspection query (only if enabled):
#   {"query":"{__schema{types{name}}}"}`],
    ['BOLA quick check', 'bash', `# as user A, request your OWN object, then your second account B's object id
curl -s https://app.example.com/api/v1/users/<A_id>/orders -H "Authorization: Bearer <A_token>"
curl -s https://app.example.com/api/v1/users/<B_id>/orders -H "Authorization: Bearer <A_token>"
# if A's token returns B's data -> BOLA (use ONLY your two accounts)`]],
  edge: [`BOLA proofs use only your own two accounts — never enumerate real users’ objects to show scale.`, `GraphQL deep-nesting and batching can DoS a server; demonstrate the primitive minimally, do not actually take the service down.`, `Introspection being enabled is often low/informative on its own; the value is using the schema to find a real auth or data bug.`, `Old API versions may be out of scope even when the current one is in scope — check.`, `APIs over-returning data means you may receive other users’ PII in a response — stop, prove with minimal data, and report; don’t collect it.`],
  faq: [['Why is BOLA the top API vulnerability?', PP(`Because REST APIs expose objects directly by identifier (<code>/users/123/orders</code>), and the server must check ownership on <i>every</i> such endpoint. New endpoints routinely ship without that check. With two accounts and ID swapping, BOLA is both extremely common and easy to prove — hence its #1 spot on the OWASP API Security Top 10.`)],
    ['Is GraphQL introspection a vulnerability?', PP(`By itself it is usually low/informative — it reveals the schema, which aids attackers but is not a breach. Its real value is as a <b>map</b>: use it to discover sensitive queries/mutations and then find missing field-level authorization, which <i>is</i> the real bug. Report introspection as context for the concrete issue you find with it.`)],
    ['How do I find the API if there are no docs?', PP(`Pull endpoints from the front-end JS (jsluice/katana), and — very productively — <b>proxy the mobile app</b> through Burp to capture its API calls, which often expose more than the web UI. Also check common doc paths (<code>/openapi.json</code>, <code>/swagger</code>, <code>/graphql</code>). The mobile backend is frequently the least-tested surface.`)]],
  qs: [SQ('What is the most common and productive API vulnerability class?', ['Clickjacking', 'BOLA — broken object-level authorization (ID swapping)', 'Missing security headers', 'Verbose errors'], 1, 'APIs address objects by ID; missing ownership checks make BOLA ubiquitous.'),
    SQ('GraphQL introspection is enabled. What is the best way to use it?', ['Report it as critical on its own', 'Use the schema to find sensitive queries/mutations with missing authorization', 'Run the deepest nested query possible to crash the server', 'Ignore it'], 1, 'Introspection is a map; the real bug is the missing field/mutation-level authorization you find with it.')]
});
DP('bb-api', {
  fu: [['What is the OWASP API Security Top 10?', 'A dedicated list for APIs, led by BOLA (object-level auth), BOPLA (property-level, incl. mass assignment and excessive data exposure), broken function-level authorization, unrestricted resource consumption, and more — the API-specific counterpart to the web Top 10.'],
    ['How do you test field-level authorization in GraphQL?', 'Request sensitive fields/mutations as a low-privilege or unauthenticated user. GraphQL resolvers often check access per operation inconsistently, so a field protected in one query may be exposed in another path — introspection helps you enumerate them all.'],
    ['Why proxy the mobile app?', 'Mobile clients talk to the same (or a broader) backend and often use endpoints the web UI never exposes, sometimes with weaker checks. Proxying the app through Burp (with the app trusting your CA) reveals this hidden, less-tested surface.']],
  deep: [['Technique', 'Import the OpenAPI/GraphQL schema to enumerate the full surface, then run two-account BOLA/BFLA tests systematically across every endpoint and method — coverage beats guesswork.'],
    ['Failure mode', 'DoSing via nested/batched GraphQL, or over-collecting data from over-returning endpoints. Prove minimally.'],
    ['In real programs', 'API BOLA and mass assignment are among the most-reported, best-paid classes; the mobile backend and old API versions are perennial soft spots.']],
  src: ['OWASP API Security Top 10 (2023)', 'https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0x11-t10/'] });
